# The Origin of the Northern Lights

Long ago, when the world was young and darkness ruled the winter skies, there lived a young weaver named Astrid in a village at the edge of the frozen north. She possessed a gift unlike any other—her threads glowed with captured starlight.

One bitter winter, the sun vanished for months, and despair settled over the land. The village elders declared that the Sky Serpent had devoured all light, condemning them to eternal darkness. But Astrid refused to surrender to hopelessness.

She climbed the highest mountain, carrying her loom and her shimmering threads. For seven days and nights, she wove a magnificent tapestry depicting all the joy, love, and hope of her people. When she finished, she cast the tapestry into the sky as an offering to the stars.

The stars were so moved by her creation that they breathed life into it. The tapestry began to dance across the heavens, rippling in waves of green, purple, and blue. The Sky Serpent, awed by such beauty, retreated forever into the depths of space.

From that night forward, Astrid's tapestry has danced across the northern skies, reminding all who witness it that even in the darkest times, hope and beauty can illuminate the world.
